"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[80317],{80317:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"content":"\\n<center>\\n<BookCard \\n          cover=\\"/assets/snippets/books/28.png\\"\\n          title=\\"28\\"\\n          author=\\"Jeong You-jeong\\"\\n          publisher=\\"Eunhaengnamu\\"\\n          publishedDate=\\"2013-06-27\\"\\n          isbn=\\"9788956607030\\"\\n          rating=4\\n          tags=\\"thriller\\"\\n        />\\n</center>\\n\\n## Review\\nA work that well shows the bare faces of humans who want to survive in a pandemic situation.  \\nThe infectious disease ```Red Eye``` spreads among dogs and begins to infect humans.  \\n<br>```Dogs``` who are slaughtered without hesitation are vividly depicted.  \\nThis book was also difficult to read because of the heavy emotional consumption.  \\nHowever, these slaughter scenes overlap with scenes of slaughtering ```humans``` in the latter half of the work.  \\nUltimately, humans are capable of slaughter regardless of <mark>species</mark> for their own survival.  \\n<br>\\nHumans who think such a disaster situation has nothing to do with them merely consume this situation.  \\n<mark>Susan Sontag\'s Regarding the Pain of Others</mark> comes to mind in this scene.  \\n<br>\\nHowever, it shows that the hope in such extreme situations is also ```human```.  \\n<br>\\nIf a zoonotic disease more fatal than foot-and-mouth disease were to spread between pets and humans, rather than cattle or pigs, would they do the same thing they did to livestock? The author\'s answer is \'yes\'.  \\n<br>\\nHumans, as the top predators of the ecosystem, are fickle tyrants.  \\n"}')}}]);